// Petal Quest — the three worlds.
//
// A level is authored as a list of **chunks**, each one 16 cells wide and 26
// cells tall, stitched left to right by `game.ptl`. Chunks are the unit a
// level is actually designed in: a chunk is half a screen, it fits on one
// editor page at full width, and a level can be re-ordered or a chunk swapped
// out without re-aligning anything.
//
// The 26 rows are the *play area*. The console's map is 30 rows; the top four
// belong to the status bar, which the scroll split holds still, so data row `r`
// is map row `r + 4` and world pixel `y = (r + 4) * 8`. Row 20 is the usual
// ground line and rows 21-25 are what is under it.
//
// Legend — terrain, drawn into the background map:
//
//   .  air          #  ground top    =  ground fill   B  brick
//   X  hard block   [  ledge left    -  ledge mid     ]  ledge right
//   *  deco A       %  deco B        ,  tuft          (  cloud/wall left
//   )  cloud/wall right               |  pole         ^  spikes (hurts)
//   F  banner (the level's goal)
//
// Legend — objects, which leave their cell empty and become entities:
//
//   @  hero spawn   o  coin          w  walker        j  jumper
//   b  flyer        H  extra life    S  star (brief invulnerability)
//   K  the boss
//
// An object's cell is where its *feet* go: a 16-tall entity written on row 19
// stands on the ground at row 20. Coins are 8x8 and sit in their cell exactly.
//
// Only the shapes are here. Which tiles those legend characters resolve to is
// `game.ptl`'s `T_*` table, and what they look like is `art.ptl` — all three
// worlds share one slot layout, so the level format never mentions a tileset.

// (One line: an `import` list may not be wrapped across lines.)
import nes: light_blue, black, navy, brown, peach, light_green, maroon, gray, light_gray, white, dark_green, green, pale_green, indigo, violet, pink, red, orange, purple, magenta, blue, cyan, pale_cyan

// Rows dull enough to deserve a name. Everything else is drawn in place.
let SKY = "................"
let GND = "################"
let FILL = "================"

// ── World 1 — Green Fields ────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The teaching level: one enemy at a time, every pit crossable at a walk, and
// the first coin arc placed where a natural jump already goes.

